2026-05-24 00:54:17,216 - INFO - Selected best state: ("python\n# Feature name: credit_amount_per_month\n# Feature description: Calculates the monthly credit repayment by dividing credit_amount by duration (in months).\n# Usefulness: This feature adds practical meaning, as it reflects the borrower's repayment burden on a monthly basis. It will likely correlate with the borrower’s ability to repay the credit, which is important for determining credit risk.\ndf['credit_amount_per_month'] = df['credit_amount'] / df['duration']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: age_group\n# Feature description: Bins the continuous age feature into categories such as young, middle_age, and senior.\n# Usefulness: Age can correlate with financial stability, employment prospects, and income. Binning simplifies the variation in the feature and enables easier interpretation of its relationship with creditworthiness. \ndf['age_group'] = pd.cut(df['age'], bins=[0, 30, 60, 100], labels=['young', 'middle_age', 'senior'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: is_short_term_loan\n# Feature description: Binary feature indicating whether the loan duration is below the first quartile (short-term loan).\n# Usefulness: Short-term loans could be indicative of less risk to lenders if they are of lower amounts or durations. Analyzing repayment risk at different loan durations may reveal important insights for downstream modeling. \ndf['is_short_term_loan'] = (df['duration'] < df['duration'].quantile(0.25)).astype(int)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column num_dependents is dropped\n# Reason: The num_dependents feature has minimal variability, since it only contains values of 1 or 2. This limited variability is unlikely to carry significant predictive power for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['num_dependents'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column other_payment_plans is dropped\n# Reason: The feature other_payment_plans contains three categories (none, bank, stores), but these distinctions might not add significant value given the other payment-related information already present (e.g., savings_status or installment_commitment).\ndf.drop(columns=['other_payment_plans'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column own_telephone is dropped\n# Reason: The own_telephone feature likely has limited predictive power in this dataset, as having a telephone (yes/no) may no longer be a relevant indicator of creditworthiness or financial status.\ndf.drop(columns=['own_telephone'], inplace=True)\nend", "Here are three possible next steps to generate new features for the downstream task:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Feature - Loan-to-Income Ratio\npython\n# loan_to_income_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of credit amount to the borrower's disposable income (approximated as disposable income inferred from installment_commitment).\n# Usefulness: This feature is critical for assessing the financial burden of the loan relative to the borrower's income. Higher values might be indicators of financial instability and could correlate strongly with loan default risk.\ndf['loan_to_income_ratio'] = df['credit_amount'] / (df['installment_commitment'] * df['credit_amount_per_month'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Feature - Employment Stability Score\npython\n# employment_stability_score\n# Feature description: Encodes employment categories into a numerical score to reflect the relative stability of each category.\n# Usefulness: Employment stability is an important factor in creditworthiness. Categories like '>=7' or '4<=X<7' likely indicate stable income, whereas 'unemployed' or '<1' might pose higher default risk. A numerical score ensures smoother relationships for modeling.\nemployment_mapping = {'>=7': 4, '4<=X<7': 3, '1<=X<4': 2, '<1': 1, 'unemployed': 0}\ndf['employment_stability_score'] = df['employment'].map(employment_mapping)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Feature - Savings-to-Loan Ratio\npython\n# savings_to_loan_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of the borrower's savings (quantifiable from 'savings_status') to the loan amount.\n# Usefulness: Borrowers with higher savings relative to their loan amount might have better financial resilience, thus lower default risk. This is important for assessing an applicant's financial preparedness for the credit amount.\nsavings_mapping = {'>=1000': 1000, '500<=X<1000': 750, '100<=X<500': 300, '<100': 50, 'no known savings': 0}\ndf['savings_amount'] = df['savings_status'].map(savings_mapping)\ndf['savings_to_loan_ratio'] = df['savings_amount'] / df['credit_amount']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns for Redundancy\nIf necessary, consider dropping columns that may be redundant or lose significance due to the generated features. For example:\n\npython\n# The 'savings_amount' column is intermediate and computed solely to generate the feature 'savings_to_loan_ratio'. \n# Dropping it after use to avoid redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['savings_amount'], inplace=True)\nend\n---\n\nThis approach captures financial health (loan-to-income and savings-to-loan ratios) and employment stability, which are critical predictors in credit risk modeling. These engineered features encode richer, more actionable insights than the original raw data."), with improvements -
